<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shosholoza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">not known (traditional; featured in the musical King Kong, <a href="/years/1959">1959</a>)</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Shosholoza&quot; is a traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> work song of the mine workers of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, in a mix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> words into which singers bring other South African languages.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The song was first sung by groups of men of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> people who travelled by steam train from their homes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, then Rhodesia, to work in the diamond and gold mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. It uses Ndebele words and is Zimbabwean in origin. Some say it describes the journey to the mines, others the return home. According to the cultural researchers Booth and Nauright, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> workers later took it up to keep the rhythm of group tasks and to ease boredom and stress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>All-male crews sang it in call and response, one man singing a line and the rest answering, as they swung their axes in time to dig. Prisoners sang it in the same way, divided by row into alto and soprano parts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> described singing it while he worked during his imprisonment on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robben-island">Robben Island</a>, calling it a song that compares the apartheid struggle to the motion of an oncoming train, and adding that the singing made the work lighter.</p><p>The word shosholoza, or tshotsholoza, means go forward or make way for the next man in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> and was a call of encouragement and solidarity among the workers. The sound sho sho imitates the steam train, stimela in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a>. Other lines include Kulezo ntaba, at those far away mountains, and Stimela siphuma eZimbabwe, the train is coming from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. The words vary from one version to another.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The song is so widely known that it is often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s second national anthem. It became more popular still after South Africa won the 1995 Rugby World Cup, when a recording sung by Dan Moyane went gold, and the national football team sang it as they came onto the field to open the 2010 FIFA World Cup. Pete Seeger, Ladysmith Black Mambazo, the Soweto Gospel Choir and Peter Gabriel are among those who have recorded it, and it was heard in the musical King Kong in 1959. Climate activists made it the centrepiece of their Occupy COP17 rally on 9 December 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shosholoza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
